<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mandalay</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mandalay is the second-largest city of Myanmar, on the east bank of the Irrawaddy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a>. It was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindon-min">King Mindon</a> in 1857 as the royal capital of the Konbaung dynasty and remained so until the British annexation of 1885. It is the country's leading centre of Buddhist learning and home to the Mahamuni Pagoda.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mandalay.jpg" alt="Mandalay" width="320" height="277" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MarenHumburg · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mandalay_-_The_worship_of_Buddha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mandalay lies on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irrawaddy-river">Irrawaddy River</a>, 631 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a>, in the central Dry Zone, at about 80 metres above sea level. It stands on the Sagaing Fault, the boundary between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> plates. An earthquake on 23 March 1839, of an estimated magnitude of 8.2, destroyed the former capital Ava and did great damage in nearby cities, and a quake of magnitude 7 in 1956 did most harm at Sagaing. The city takes its name from Mandalay Hill, probably from a Pali word, perhaps mandala, a circular plain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindon-min">King Mindon</a> founded the royal city on 13 February 1857 at the foot of Mandalay Hill, ostensibly to fulfil a prophecy that a great city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> would be founded on that spot at the 2,400th jubilee of Buddhism. Its official name was Yadanarbon, from the Pali Ratanapura, meaning City of Gems. It was the last royal capital before the British annexation of 1885, after which commercial and political importance moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a>. The British looted the palace, and part of the treasure went on display in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victoria-and-albert-museum">Victoria and Albert Museum</a> until it was returned in 1964. A railway reached Mandalay in 1889, but its first college was founded only in 1925.</p><p>The city was extensively destroyed in the Japanese conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and most of the palace was burnt, a replica standing there now. After independence in 1948 Mandalay remained the main cultural, educational and economic centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a>. Fires in the 1980s cleared large areas, and from the 1990s a large migration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a>, mostly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, changed the ethnic makeup of the city and its commerce. Its population doubled from about 500,000 in 1980 to one million in 2008.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mandalay is the second-largest city of Myanmar, with 1,225,553 inhabitants in 2014, and the economic centre of Upper Myanmar. It is regarded as the centre of Burmese culture and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, with numerous monasteries and more than 700 pagodas. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuthodaw-pagoda">Kuthodaw Pagoda</a> at the foot of Mandalay Hill, 729 stone slabs carry the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pali-canon">Pali Canon</a>, each in its own white stupa. Traditional industries include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> weaving, jade cutting, stone and wood carving, and the making of bronze and marble Buddha images.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mandalay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At Mindon’s Kuthodaw Pagoda in <b>Mandalay</b> the entire Pali Buddhist canon is carved on 729 marble slabs, each housed in its own small shrine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…became independent as the Union of Burma on 4 January 1948, ending more than six decades of British rule that had begun with the annexation of Arakan and Tenasserim in 1826 and of Lower Burma in 1852 and the fall of the Konbaung kingdom at <b>Mandalay</b> in 1885.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By 1500 BCE people in the dry zone near the Irrawaddy worked bronze, grew rice and kept chickens and pigs, and by 500 BCE iron-working settlements in the Samon valley south of <b>Mandalay</b> traded with China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pyu-city-states">Pyu city-states · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bagan Archaeological Zone covers an area of 13 km by 8 km on the bend of the Irrawaddy, 290 km south-west of <b>Mandalay</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/bagan">Bagan · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Its video was taken from a television special filmed at the <b>Mandalay</b> nightclub in Newmarket, Auckland, and it was later included in Nature's Best 3, a collection of the top 100 New Zealand songs as voted by members of APRA.</blockquote><a href="/songs/e-ipo">E Ipo</a></li><li><blockquote>The saopha of Hsipaw ruled from a haw, or palace, overseeing rice and tea growing and trade along the road from <b>Mandalay</b> to China.</blockquote><a href="/state/hsipaw-state">Hsipaw State</a></li></ul></section>
